{"packet_id": "6683ae065fbe2ccb", "dimensions": {"problem_framing": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A1", "A2", "A3"], "reason": "A1 lists obvious options, including licensing and staying B2B, and states an objective. It does not separate the objective from the suggested solution in any useful way, and it never corrects the premise. Alternative 3 frames the status quo as 'continue focusing exclusively on B2B'. But sources found in A2/A3 (jotun-pci-diy, jotun-pci-yachts, jotun-boatnews-direct) show Jotun already serves DIY consumers and sells its leisure-yacht range directly to individuals. The specific use case 'residential marine safety' is introduced without grounding."}, "causal_mechanism": {"status": "scored", "score": 0, "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"], "reason": "The best theory (T1, A5) offers nonsense as its mechanism: consumers buying boat coating as a 'medical necessity'. Nothing explains why a coating would be a health product or how marine technology maps onto that need. T2 (A6) is equally ungrounded ('marine safety as a status signal'). This is slogan-level reasoning, not a behaviour chain leading to value captured."}, "distinct_theories": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"], "reason": "T1 and T2 share the same use case, customer, channel and outcome. They differ only in the messaging frame (safety versus fashion), so they are rewordings of one idea. No rival implies a different product, customer group or channel, such as leisure-yacht DIY or extending the decorative range."}, "assumptions_predictions": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"], "reason": "Some predictions are nominally observable (WTP at least 20% or 15% above a generic product, purchase intent). However, the core assumption that consumers see a coating as a medical necessity is never operationalised. The thresholds are arbitrary assumptions. Repeat-purchase predictions cannot be observed in a survey. A regulatory assumption ('will increasingly require') is vague. The predictions do not tie failure back to the theory's stated mechanism."}, "evidence_judgement": {"status": "scored", "score": 0, "event_ids": ["A7", "A8", "A9", "A10", "A11"], "reason": "A7 marks jotun-boatnews-direct as supporting T1. That source is a company claim about direct online sales and being 'cheaper than the competition'. This cuts against T1's premium-pricing mechanism, and the 'does not show' note concedes it says nothing about safety or WTP. The sources retrieved in A9-A11 (soft Scandinavian decorative demand, modest decorative growth, existing yacht coatings) are never assessed. No date, place or segment limits are discussed."}, "seeking_challenge": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A9", "A13"], "reason": "A9 and A13 search against T1 and T2. Their results (jotun-q1-2024-soft, jotun-q2-2024-modest, jotun-pci-yachts) are never reported, assessed or weighed. The soft consumer demand in Scandinavia is directly relevant to a Scandinavian consumer launch, yet it is silently ignored."}, "experiment_design": {"status": "scored", "score": 1, "event_ids": ["A12"], "reason": "A12 is concrete on sample and cost, but its logic is broken:
